In `StateManagerMemory.modify_state`, the first thing it does is normalize the incoming token with `_split_substate_key`, then inside the lock it calls `await self.get_state(token)`, and after the `yield` it calls `await self.set_state(token, state)`. `get_state` itself also begins by calling `_split_substate_key` before looking up or creating the top-level state. In the test, the `async with manager.modify_state("client-token_child")` block therefore produces `modify_state -> _split_substate_key -> get_state -> _split_substate_key`, and when the context exits it resumes the same `modify_state` call and then invokes `set_state`. After that, line 22 directly calls `manager.get_state("client-token_other")`, which again calls `_split_substate_key`, and line 23 directly calls `manager.set_state("client-token", same_state)`. Since `set_state` is just `pass`, it contributes only its own call entries.

